our agenda. We're going over to chair Karan

01:25:46.130 --> 01:25:49.720
for item. 11, the Administrative Development

01:25:50.220 --> 01:25:53.720
Charge Bylaw Update. I'll turn things over to you. Okay, thank you, Chair Goller.

01:25:54.220 --> 01:25:57.320
We've got two fairly major items today, and that is the Administrative

01:25:57.820 --> 01:26:00.920
Development Charge Bylaw and the property tax policy.

01:26:01.160 --> 01:26:04.480
So I'm going to turn it over. We have a presentation for the first

01:26:04.980 --> 01:26:10.120
item, Shanna O'Driscoll and Justin Way in our finance

01:26:10.620 --> 01:26:12.680
department. Over to you for our presentation.

01:26:20.530 --> 01:26:23.850
Good morning, members of council. My name is Justin Way and I'm the manager of

01:26:24.350 --> 01:26:25.330
financial strategy and reporting.

01:26:27.970 --> 01:26:31.890
I'm pleased to be before you today to present an overview of the administrative updates

01:26:32.390 --> 01:26:35.810
to the DC bylaw that are recommended in the staff reports. I'll then turn

01:26:36.310 --> 01:26:39.170
it over to Shanna to provide an update on what other municipalities are doing with

01:26:39.470 --> 01:26:40.350
with DC incentives.

01:26:45.630 --> 01:26:48.910
So over the past 20 months or so, there have been three major pieces of

01:26:49.410 --> 01:26:52.152
legislation that have impacted the DC Act. There are bills 185,

01:26:52.228 --> 01:26:55.910
bill 17, bill 60. If you recall, our DC bylaw

01:26:56.410 --> 01:27:00.120
was last updated in November 2024 for some of the changes made through bill

01:27:00.220 --> 01:27:03.950
185 that had a special transitional pathway and timeline.

01:27:04.270 --> 01:27:07.590
The remaining change related to updating the rate freeze provisions is being

01:27:08.090 --> 01:27:11.610
made through this update. As it was not included in that previous special process.

01:27:12.250 --> 01:27:15.722
The second item is Bill 17, which received Royal Assent in June

01:27:15.818 --> 01:27:19.344
2025. This was followed by an order in council on October

01:27:19.436 --> 01:27:22.490
23 for changes in effect as of November 3.

01:27:23.450 --> 01:27:26.890
And the impact of this was the deferral of DC payment timing from building permit

01:27:27.390 --> 01:27:31.010
issuance to occupancy. Then lastly, Bill 60, which received Royal

01:27:31.510 --> 01:27:35.770
Assent in November 2025. While there are a few administrative

01:27:36.270 --> 01:27:39.640
updates that go into effect right away, most of those changes and

01:27:40.140 --> 01:27:41.240
impacts require some future work ahead.

01:27:45.080 --> 01:27:48.760
So the DC bylaw update before council for approval today

01:27:49.260 --> 01:27:53.120
is administrative in nature. It primarily includes changes that align

01:27:53.620 --> 01:27:57.720
our DC bylaw with legislation, some minor housekeeping items to clarify definitions,

01:27:57.880 --> 01:28:01.480
and it does not alter growth forecasts, capital requirements, or any DC

01:28:01.980 --> 01:28:05.440
rates. The first change is related to Bill 185, which limits

01:28:05.940 --> 01:28:09.730
the timeline for a DC rate freeze to 18 months, Prior to this, DC rates

01:28:10.230 --> 01:28:14.010
were frozen at the time of a complete site plan application or zoning bylaw amendments

01:28:14.510 --> 01:28:17.290
and they were frozen from two years from that date of application approval.

01:28:17.930 --> 01:28:21.610
Going forward, they'll be frozen for 18 months. Interest continues to accrue

01:28:22.110 --> 01:28:25.210
on the frozen DC rates from the time of the planning application and that has

01:28:25.710 --> 01:28:29.090
not changed. And really the objective of this change is to speed up development once

01:28:29.590 --> 01:28:32.770
planning approvals are in place. The second and biggest change

01:28:33.270 --> 01:28:36.460
was from Bill 17 which has updated the timing of payments from building permit

01:28:36.960 --> 01:28:40.380
issuance to occupancy. And this will have a substantial impact on the timing of our

01:28:40.880 --> 01:28:44.340
DC collections and we'll look into that estimate of this impact on the next slide.

01:28:44.820 --> 01:28:48.180
It's also important to note as interest is no longer to be charged from the

01:28:48.680 --> 01:28:52.220
DC's owing from building permit dates to occupancy. We see

01:28:52.720 --> 01:28:56.420
the time value of money also erode from this change. Nevertheless, the building

01:28:56.920 --> 01:29:00.340
community strongly advocated for this change as it will reduce the carrying charges they

01:29:00.840 --> 01:29:02.600
have on the developments ultimately improving the performance.

01:29:06.680 --> 01:29:10.640
So looking at the financial impact at payment at occupancy, there's several items

01:29:11.140 --> 01:29:15.120
to note. The delayed payment timing will result in cash flow pressures

01:29:15.620 --> 01:29:19.680
for the city, decreased present value of our DC revenues, lost interest earnings or greater

01:29:20.180 --> 01:29:24.400
interest costs from increased requirements to debt finance and greater uncertainty in timing

01:29:24.900 --> 01:29:28.520
your receipt. For this analysis, the finance team worked closely with the building team to

01:29:29.020 --> 01:29:32.890
pull data on the average number of months between building permit issuance and occupancy

01:29:33.290 --> 01:29:36.650
for residential development at the city by type. So the first

01:29:37.150 --> 01:29:40.490
table on the left shows that the average ranges for from nine months,

01:29:40.730 --> 01:29:44.250
17 months and 14 months for low, medium and high density respectively.

01:29:44.650 --> 01:29:48.930
We then apply this data to the forecast DC collections, which was prepared previously

01:29:49.430 --> 01:29:53.090
when collection was at building permit issuance. So with

01:29:53.590 --> 01:29:56.770
the chart on the right, you'll see that this estimated impact will be in the

01:29:57.270 --> 01:30:00.690
range of 10 to 20 million over the next three years. Staff will

01:30:01.190 --> 01:30:04.250
continue to track and report on this impact through our quarterly budget monitoring reports,

01:30:04.810 --> 01:30:08.850
but it's very likely that additional capital deferrals will be required through future capital

01:30:09.350 --> 01:30:11.290
budget updates in order to manage these impacts.

01:30:14.010 --> 01:30:17.490
And then lastly, we'll look at Bill 60. So there's two

01:30:17.990 --> 01:30:21.090
changes effective immediately upon royal assent,

01:30:21.590 --> 01:30:25.090
and these are primarily related to reporting and oversight. So firstly, there's a new

01:30:25.590 --> 01:30:29.150
requirement to have the annual DC statements go to council by June 30th and submit

01:30:29.650 --> 01:30:33.070
it to the minister by July 15th. Secondly, the minister may request copies

01:30:33.570 --> 01:30:36.390
of our DC bylaws and background studies at any time. Overall,

01:30:36.890 --> 01:30:40.230
happy to report these changes are not a concern as we're already producing these documents

01:30:40.730 --> 01:30:44.150
within those required timelines. So we have no problem submitting them to the province

01:30:44.310 --> 01:30:47.910
through the required deadlines. And then there's two major changes,

01:30:48.410 --> 01:30:51.670
sorry, two main changes that require some future work ahead for the city. First,

01:30:52.170 --> 01:30:55.840
local service policy or LSPs. So Bill 60 introduced a

01:30:56.340 --> 01:30:59.920
requirement to include LSP as part of the DC background study. While some

01:31:00.420 --> 01:31:05.080
municipalities don't have LSP in place, Guelph's DC background study does and only

01:31:05.580 --> 01:31:08.760
minor updates would be needed to bring it fully into compliance with the new requirements.

01:31:09.320 --> 01:31:13.120
However, the city's last major review and update of the LSP occurred with

01:31:13.620 --> 01:31:16.840
the 2018 DC background study, so staff are recommending a more fulsome review

01:31:17.080 --> 01:31:21.050
and update to be completed and brought back to council for approval by May 2027.

01:31:21.610 --> 01:31:25.530
And that timeline's in alignment with the transition timelines provided for in the legislation.

01:31:26.090 --> 01:31:29.690
And really this review would help identify any opportunities to improve

01:31:30.190 --> 01:31:33.770
clarity and predictability around the LSP and ultimately support the next comprehensive

01:31:34.270 --> 01:31:38.010
DC background study update, which is currently planned to begin in 2027 for council

01:31:38.510 --> 01:31:41.850
approval in 2029. Secondly, it's a

01:31:42.350 --> 01:31:45.810
new land acquisition class. So Bill 60 introduced a requirement to separate land

01:31:46.310 --> 01:31:49.750
costs into a separate class of service in DC bylaws and as part of

01:31:50.250 --> 01:31:54.030
this to remove land values from the historic service level standards for each individual service.

01:31:55.310 --> 01:31:58.230
And the transition timelines require this work to be done as part of the next

01:31:58.730 --> 01:32:03.230
comprehensive DC background study update, which as mentioned is slated to begin in 2027

01:32:03.870 --> 01:32:07.590
and coming to council in 2029. And with that, I'll hand it over to

01:32:08.090 --> 01:32:08.590
Shanna for the last few slides.

01:32:14.040 --> 01:32:16.200
Thank you Justin and good morning Council.

01:32:17.240 --> 01:32:20.840
So these days there is always a lot going on with development charges

01:32:21.000 --> 01:32:24.920
and that includes legislative changes, lobbying by the building community,

01:32:25.320 --> 01:32:28.640
analysis being undertaken by think tanks and initiatives being

01:32:29.140 --> 01:32:32.440
undertaken by municipalities. So while we're in front of you on

01:32:32.940 --> 01:32:36.000
this topic we wanted to take the opportunity to update you on what we're seeing

01:32:36.500 --> 01:32:37.640
out there from other municipalities.

01:32:45.570 --> 01:32:48.930
There we go. So last year we brought you information

01:32:49.090 --> 01:32:52.450
in the March Housing and Guelph semi-annual update about what some

01:32:52.950 --> 01:32:57.010
municipalities had done with respect to providing discounts or exemptions

01:32:57.510 --> 01:33:01.410
from DCS. Since then some of those discounts have been expanded and

01:33:01.910 --> 01:33:05.290
others have begun to explore incentives. For example, last year

01:33:05.790 --> 01:33:09.770
we reported to you that Mississauga had partially discounted their DC rates and

01:33:10.270 --> 01:33:12.890
since then they have expanded that to a full grant in lieu for some types

01:33:13.390 --> 01:33:16.650
of residential building. Peel Region has matched this initiative

01:33:17.150 --> 01:33:19.490
with both set to expire at the end of this council term.

01:33:20.210 --> 01:33:23.970
The City of London is currently in the process of exploring a community improvement

01:33:24.470 --> 01:33:27.850
plan to provide support for first-time home buyers and last fall the

01:33:28.350 --> 01:33:32.520
City of Burlington directed their staff to bring back options for DC

01:33:33.020 --> 01:33:36.520
incentives. These approaches vary in scope, duration and

01:33:37.020 --> 01:33:40.480
fiscal exposure. Some have funding commitments from other levels of

01:33:40.980 --> 01:33:43.400
government to support their changes and others do not.

01:33:44.200 --> 01:33:47.800
We are providing this update to let you know that we are monitoring what others

01:33:47.880 --> 01:33:51.360
are doing and why we do not recommend going down this path

01:33:51.860 --> 01:33:52.120
in Guelph.

01:33:57.330 --> 01:34:00.930
As a reminder, DC rates are set to recover the capital costs

01:34:01.430 --> 01:34:04.690
of infrastructure that support growth within legislated constraints.

01:34:05.170 --> 01:34:09.010
The rates are a mathematical calculation that spreads the eligible capital

01:34:09.510 --> 01:34:12.650
costs across forecasted growth. Providing an

01:34:13.150 --> 01:34:16.450
exemption from paying DCs creates a gap because it does not

01:34:16.950 --> 01:34:20.580
make the infrastructure cost less. It simply provides less funds to

01:34:21.080 --> 01:34:24.780
pay for it. The exemption gap must be filled and municipalities

01:34:25.280 --> 01:34:28.980
have very limited options for how to do that, namely property taxes

01:34:29.480 --> 01:34:33.500
and utility rates. Guelph has highly prioritized growth enabling

01:34:34.000 --> 01:34:37.420
capital works in our capital budget and forecast, even in the face

01:34:37.920 --> 01:34:41.780
of low DC collections using debt financing and inter-reserve fund

01:34:42.280 --> 01:34:45.980
borrowing. This means that the city is committed to DC debt payments

01:34:46.530 --> 01:34:49.810
that non-eligible capital work has been deferred to accommodate

01:34:50.310 --> 01:34:54.090
inter-reserve fund borrowing. This puts pressure on older infrastructure

01:34:54.590 --> 01:34:58.330
and increases the risk of service disruption, and that is a trade-off for

01:34:58.830 --> 01:35:02.529
moving infrastructure that supports growth forward in the face of limited capital

01:35:02.530 --> 01:35:06.250
funds. There are also significant statutory

01:35:06.750 --> 01:35:09.770
discounts and exemptions already required, targeted at

01:35:10.270 --> 01:35:13.370
development that the province has prioritized. Rental housing,

01:35:13.530 --> 01:35:17.050
affordable and non-profit housing, and additional dwelling units.

01:35:17.610 --> 01:35:21.450
Making up for these exemptions is already diverting a significant amount of

01:35:21.950 --> 01:35:25.570
property tax and utility rate supported capital funding to

01:35:26.070 --> 01:35:30.490
help pay for growth. The development community has been very active

01:35:30.990 --> 01:35:34.370
in advocating for DC related changes and reductions and have

01:35:34.870 --> 01:35:38.610
become very effective at it. They recently successfully advocated for

01:35:39.110 --> 01:35:42.060
moving payment of residential DCs from building permit to occupancy.

01:35:42.290 --> 01:35:45.650
As you just heard from Justin. This has been in place since

01:35:46.150 --> 01:35:50.050
November 3rd, but so far we have not seen this result in increased building

01:35:50.550 --> 01:35:52.530
permits. We are hopeful that will change.

01:35:58.850 --> 01:36:02.210
Zeroing in on Burlington for a moment, this slide shows what

01:36:02.710 --> 01:36:06.090
services are included in Guelph's single tier DC versus

01:36:06.590 --> 01:36:08.880
Burlington's lower tier and Halton's upper tier DC.

01:36:09.990 --> 01:36:13.110
Burlington has not finalised their direction forward

01:36:13.610 --> 01:36:17.110
at this time, but one option that was moved forward at a vote

01:36:17.610 --> 01:36:21.030
of their committee of the whole in February was a broad-based residential DC

01:36:21.530 --> 01:36:24.789
exemption. Earlier this week, they voted instead to direct

01:36:25.289 --> 01:36:28.830
their staff to pursue a potential CIP option with funding

01:36:29.330 --> 01:36:33.470
from another source aside from property taxes. While Burlington's

01:36:33.970 --> 01:36:37.150
ultimate decision on this matter is uncertain, we wanted to show you some of the

01:36:37.650 --> 01:36:41.110
differences between Guelph and Burlington. A Burlington exemption

01:36:41.610 --> 01:36:45.510
would only apply to the lower tier portion of the DC. The Halton

01:36:46.010 --> 01:36:49.350
Region DC would still be applicable to development in Burlington and even

01:36:49.850 --> 01:36:53.590
with a full lower tier exemption, the cost of building a single detached

01:36:54.090 --> 01:36:58.110
residential unit in Burlington would be just under $7,000 less than

01:36:58.610 --> 01:37:01.990
the undiscounted rate in Guelph. The other thing to point out

01:37:02.490 --> 01:37:06.310
is that some of the core enabling services like water, wastewater and stormwater

01:37:06.700 --> 01:37:10.620
would not be impacted as those services are provided at the regional level.

01:37:11.900 --> 01:37:15.700
So what if Guelph did that? Based on planning application

01:37:16.200 --> 01:37:19.780
status, there are 654 units ready and fairly likely to move

01:37:20.280 --> 01:37:24.140
ahead to building permit in 2026 and 871 more

01:37:24.640 --> 01:37:28.780
units with the potential to do so. If Guelph implemented a blanket

01:37:29.280 --> 01:37:32.540
residential DC exemption similar to that which Burlington was considering

01:37:33.040 --> 01:37:37.210
at one point, That would require municipal tax and rate payers to make up over

01:37:37.710 --> 01:37:41.570
$46 million in DC exemptions, a property tax impact

01:37:42.070 --> 01:37:45.890
of 8.85%, plus increases to water rates.

01:37:46.130 --> 01:37:49.769
And that would be in addition to the nearly $18 million of statutory

01:37:50.269 --> 01:37:53.650
DC exemptions and discounts that would already need to be funded

01:37:54.130 --> 01:37:57.690
for the rental and affordable or non-profit units included in

01:37:58.190 --> 01:37:58.850
those estimates.

01:38:04.860 --> 01:38:08.180
So this is a chart that you've seen before and this version was updated for

01:38:08.680 --> 01:38:11.980
us last August by Watson and Associates. The green arrow

01:38:12.480 --> 01:38:16.380
shows you where Guelph's residential DCs are in comparison with other municipalities

01:38:16.780 --> 01:38:20.220
all in including the school board DCs. We felt

01:38:20.720 --> 01:38:24.140
it was important to show this given recent inaccurate reports that Guelph's

01:38:24.640 --> 01:38:27.270
DCs are at the higher end. That is simply not true.

01:38:32.950 --> 01:38:36.710
The reality is that limited municipal revenue tools combined

01:38:37.210 --> 01:38:40.710
with a high level of responsibility for infrastructure is

01:38:41.210 --> 01:38:44.750
creating an untenable financial situation. If DCs

01:38:45.250 --> 01:38:49.110
are reduced, the city's options are to defer growth infrastructure

01:38:49.610 --> 01:38:53.590
investment indefinitely, constraining growth and working against our responsibility

01:38:53.750 --> 01:38:57.260
to build infrastructure to enable to remove the growth targets to be

01:38:57.760 --> 01:39:01.300
met, or to remove amenity-based infrastructure from

01:39:01.800 --> 01:39:05.780
our DCS and only deliver core infrastructure, which puts pressure

01:39:06.280 --> 01:39:10.620
on the municipal infrastructure that makes a community, or shift additional

01:39:11.120 --> 01:39:14.940
costs to the property tax and rate base. Over the past several years,

01:39:15.440 --> 01:39:19.460
the province has made dozens of changes to D.C. and planning legislation.

01:39:20.260 --> 01:39:24.340
Every time it requires analysis, quantification of impacts,

01:39:24.830 --> 01:39:28.350
reporting, communication, adjustments to financial strategies,

01:39:28.750 --> 01:39:32.590
adjustments to administrative processes and systems, and bylaw updates.

01:39:33.550 --> 01:39:37.070
Every time there's an announcement from any level of government about an

01:39:37.570 --> 01:39:41.390
intent to look into or do something related to reduced DCS

01:39:41.470 --> 01:39:44.750
or other changes to make development conditions more favorable,

01:39:45.150 --> 01:39:49.110
the effect is to freeze up development plans until that policy is

01:39:49.610 --> 01:39:51.960
either implemented or determined not to be moving forward.

01:39:53.000 --> 01:39:56.760
A new approach is needed. Attempting to incentivize development

01:39:56.920 --> 01:40:00.760
through constant legislative changes has not had the desired effect.

01:40:01.400 --> 01:40:05.120
We need an approach that is administratively efficient and does not place

01:40:05.620 --> 01:40:08.520
all the risk and financial burden on municipalities.

01:40:09.480 --> 01:40:13.920
We need new, stable, predictable funding for growth infrastructure

01:40:14.420 --> 01:40:18.650
from other upper levels of government. I have emphasized or underlined

01:40:19.150 --> 01:40:22.690
the word new on the slide because I don't mean the repurposing of existing

01:40:23.190 --> 01:40:26.730
infrastructure funding such as federal gas tax, which we already rely

01:40:27.230 --> 01:40:31.290
on for capital infrastructure renewal work. Stable and predictable

01:40:31.790 --> 01:40:35.170
means a long-term commitment to annual allocations that we do

01:40:35.670 --> 01:40:39.450
not have to submit applications for with uncertain outcomes and unknown

01:40:39.950 --> 01:40:43.770
approval timelines. Upfront means that upper levels of government do

01:40:44.270 --> 01:40:48.460
not require municipalities to take on risky decisions to provide time

01:40:48.700 --> 01:40:52.900
limited exemptions underwritten by property tax and rate payers and

01:40:53.400 --> 01:40:57.740
hope for that money to come. It means new dedicated

01:40:58.240 --> 01:41:01.260
funding for growth infrastructure that municipalities can count on.

01:41:01.740 --> 01:41:04.940
With that kind of commitment, we could update our DC study,

01:41:05.500 --> 01:41:09.580
reduce DC rates without jeopardizing our capital plans and

01:41:10.080 --> 01:41:13.750
without pushing the cost onto property taxpayers. So that's

01:41:14.390 --> 01:41:18.110
everything I have to share, and we're happy to take any questions

01:41:18.610 --> 01:41:22.470
from council. Thank you

01:41:22.970 --> 01:41:26.110
so much for that presentation. And that call to action for, for legislative

01:41:26.610 --> 01:41:29.830
stability was really, really hit. I was, I'm glad you added that to your

01:41:30.330 --> 01:41:33.590
slide as, as a, a call to action at the end.

01:41:34.390 --> 01:41:38.590
Before we go to council questions, I recognise, uh, Councillor alt

01:41:39.090 --> 01:41:43.270
as as the first question. We are under a public meeting under

01:41:43.770 --> 01:41:46.870
Section 12 of the Development Charges Act, so I do need to call for anyone

01:41:47.370 --> 01:41:51.150
in the audience if you are here to delegate and there

01:41:51.650 --> 01:41:54.550
will be a number going up on the screen if there's anyone watching from home

01:41:54.630 --> 01:41:58.110
who would like to delegate on this matter. Now is the time to call

01:41:58.610 --> 01:42:01.990
in, so we'll take a few minutes to pause and

01:42:02.490 --> 01:42:05.790
wait for any calls to come in. Is there anyone in the gallery

01:42:06.290 --> 01:42:10.380
who wishes to to delegate on this item. Seeing none, we'll just

01:42:10.880 --> 01:42:11.300
wait one moment.

01:42:46.350 --> 01:42:49.150
We've had no incoming calls, so we're going to move to discussion.

01:42:49.470 --> 01:42:52.870
Councillor Alt, you have the floor. Thank you very

01:42:53.370 --> 01:42:56.910
much, Chair Karan. Through you to our staff

01:42:57.410 --> 01:43:00.562
and in particular to Ms.

01:43:00.658 --> 01:43:03.090
O'Dwyer with first of all a shout out.

01:43:04.610 --> 01:43:07.668
I want to compliment our staff and particularly Ms.

01:43:07.752 --> 01:43:11.090
O'Dwyer on perhaps the most blatantly political

01:43:11.410 --> 01:43:15.250
financial report that I have ever seen at city

01:43:15.750 --> 01:43:19.610
council and one that is both direct and forthright and founded

01:43:20.110 --> 01:43:23.890
in fact. Ms. O'Dwyer, I think you might be my first

01:43:24.210 --> 01:43:28.010
financial bean counter hero. It's something that we need to

01:43:28.510 --> 01:43:31.950
hear and it's something that every municipality in Ontario needs to state

01:43:32.830 --> 01:43:36.390
because Doug Ford does it again. With that,

01:43:36.890 --> 01:43:39.910
I'd like to go to some of the particulars. I originally only had one question,

01:43:40.410 --> 01:43:43.950
but as the report went on, more grew. First of

01:43:44.450 --> 01:43:48.590
all, I'd like to focus on Bill 17 and interest versus carrying charges.

01:43:49.230 --> 01:43:53.070
Some comments were made about capital deferrals, and this strikes me as a

01:43:53.570 --> 01:43:57.310
vicious cycle that we're now entering into. Could we please comment

01:43:57.550 --> 01:44:01.160
on what kind of capital deferrals we might have to

01:44:01.400 --> 01:44:04.920
address. Will these be infrastructure and will these,

01:44:05.000 --> 01:44:09.080
interestingly enough, therefore lead to fewer

01:44:09.580 --> 01:44:13.640
building permits possibly being approved because

01:44:14.040 --> 01:44:18.520
we just don't have the infrastructure? I don't know whether that's to our financial

01:44:18.600 --> 01:44:21.240
staff or whether our planning staff needs to address it.

01:44:24.440 --> 01:44:29.100
Through the chair, I'll start. And so I think the

01:44:29.500 --> 01:44:33.100
level of deferrals of capital works through

01:44:33.600 --> 01:44:36.620
the next budget cycle and future budget cycles will really be dependent

01:44:36.940 --> 01:44:40.700
on how those estimates bear out in reality in

01:44:41.200 --> 01:44:45.340
terms of the delayed collections and how that impacts what we

01:44:45.580 --> 01:44:49.940
had projected. And so we will take the

01:44:50.440 --> 01:44:54.960
usual methodical approach that we take to updating the capital forecast we'll

01:44:55.460 --> 01:44:58.880
update those projections, we will make other

01:44:59.380 --> 01:45:03.360
changes if we get any grants, or if we end

01:45:03.860 --> 01:45:07.720
up closing out capital projects under budget and are able to return money

01:45:08.220 --> 01:45:11.720
to the reserve funds. So all of that will factor into that next update.

01:45:12.200 --> 01:45:15.720
And then we will rely on our capital prioritization framework

01:45:16.440 --> 01:45:20.700
to figure out which projects to push

01:45:21.200 --> 01:45:24.900
out. Thank you. I wonder if you could comment on

01:45:25.400 --> 01:45:28.780
what I consider to be the incredible irony that this could actually

01:45:29.340 --> 01:45:33.380
lead to a hesitation of

01:45:33.880 --> 01:45:36.940
builders to actually consider large-scale developments because

01:45:37.440 --> 01:45:41.380
the infrastructure or other amenities just aren't there. Is that

01:45:41.880 --> 01:45:45.660
a correct assumption? And is the promise of the 3 million

01:45:45.900 --> 01:45:50.190
homes actually that we've heard really

01:45:50.690 --> 01:45:51.630
just dust in the wind.

01:45:55.070 --> 01:45:59.230
Thank you through the chair. I'll start and then others to my left may have

01:45:59.550 --> 01:46:03.789
thoughts on that. I think that absolutely there's

01:46:04.289 --> 01:46:06.910
a cycle there to look at with infrastructure.

01:46:08.190 --> 01:46:11.990
We need to build enabling infrastructure to allow development to happen

01:46:12.490 --> 01:46:15.710
and we're doing our best to move that forward. In the face of all of

01:46:16.210 --> 01:46:18.630
these changes, recognising the need for that development.

01:46:19.590 --> 01:46:23.590
But there is a time that will come that high priority

01:46:24.090 --> 01:46:27.990
items, we've deferred all the medium priority.

01:46:28.630 --> 01:46:32.350
We started to defer some of the medium high priority

01:46:32.850 --> 01:46:37.270
last year, and so there's not that much further to go in the future besides

01:46:37.770 --> 01:46:41.560
high priority deferrals, I think. There's absolutely a

01:46:42.060 --> 01:46:46.480
circular effect that will happen. Now we do have our municipal

01:46:46.980 --> 01:46:50.840
service and financing agreements policy in place so that if we've

01:46:51.340 --> 01:46:55.000
deferred infrastructure out in our capital budget but a developer is ready to

01:46:55.500 --> 01:46:59.000
move something forward ahead of that timeline, there is

01:46:59.500 --> 01:47:03.360
a pathway for them to do that. And so we've tried to balance

01:47:03.860 --> 01:47:07.570
that impact. Are there

01:47:08.070 --> 01:47:10.170
comments from the other side? I don't know. I can't see.

01:47:13.050 --> 01:47:17.250
Through the chair, I think I would just say that in

01:47:17.750 --> 01:47:22.050
terms of the prioritization already done, we were prioritizing citywide

01:47:22.550 --> 01:47:27.970
projects that would advance citywide growth rather

01:47:28.470 --> 01:47:31.850
than site specific. And so I think that that would

01:47:32.350 --> 01:47:35.650
be the first place that we would see a bit of a

01:47:36.150 --> 01:47:40.080
change is that we would we would potentially

01:47:40.580 --> 01:47:43.920
be impacting specific developments if we had to go any further than we are

01:47:44.420 --> 01:47:48.360
right now. So hopefully that that helps kind

01:47:48.860 --> 01:47:52.120
of round out the question. If I might carry

01:47:52.620 --> 01:47:56.440
that on then, quite often there are

01:47:58.680 --> 01:48:02.120
armchair quarterbacks, I guess I'll say, who are constantly

01:48:02.620 --> 01:48:06.080
talking about libraries and rec centres. We're not talking libraries and

01:48:06.580 --> 01:48:09.860
rec centres here, are we? We're talking about the very foundations of

01:48:10.360 --> 01:48:11.060
building it. Are we not?

01:48:15.220 --> 01:48:17.780
Through the mayor or sorry, rather the chair,

01:48:18.500 --> 01:48:22.340
we are talking about all of the infrastructure that

01:48:22.840 --> 01:48:26.500
